{
    "title": "Fisheries Off West Coast States; Modification of the West Coast Salmon Fisheries; Inseason Actions #1-#5",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 22, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-22/pdf/2025-23634.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register brings three notable updates: a Labor Department rule change that lowers costs for some small businesses, a temporary safety zone on the Sacramento River for New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks, and new, real-time adjustments to ocean salmon fishing that include an early spring closure north of Cape Falcon, Oregon.\n\nThe Labor Department removed a set of regulations tied to federal service contracting, estimating net cost savings of about $4.28 million for small firms. Supporters will see this as a cut in red tape and compliance costs for companies that work on government jobs. Critics may worry it reduces protections for workers on those contracts, potentially affecting job stability when contracts change hands.\n\nIn Sacramento, the Coast Guard will enforce a short, evening safety zone on December 31 around the Delta King Hotel near the Tower Bridge. This is meant to keep people safe during the fireworks display by keeping unauthorized boats out of the area for just over an hour. Boaters and others on the water may find the temporary restriction inconvenient, but the intent is to prevent accidents during a crowded holiday event.\n\nOn the West Coast, federal fishery managers adjusted the commercial salmon season between the United States\u2013Canada border and Cape Falcon. After early catches ran high, limits were tightened; when poor weather slowed fishing, limits were eased; and as catch rates rose again, limits were reduced and the spring season north of Cape Falcon was closed on June 18 to avoid exceeding the quota. Conservation and long-term stock health stand to benefit from staying within set limits, and the step-by-step changes aim to share opportunity more fairly across areas. At the same time, commercial fishers, processors, and coastal communities may feel the strain from shifting limits and the early closure, which can disrupt income and planning.",
